	•	Section 5 Comments/Quotes: The outcome was high-cost hospitalization, defined as total hospital charges at or above the cohort’s 80th percentile ($600,223.40). On the held-out test set, GBM achieved AUC 0.8078, accuracy 0.8164, precision 0.6944, recall 0.6657, and F1-score 0.6773. No AUC confidence interval, explicitly reported specificity, or NPV was provided. Calibration curves were presented, and GBM had a Brier score of 0.1360. Decision-curve analysis was not reported.

Section 6: Quality Assessment (Risk of Bias / PROBAST)
	•	Participants (Selection Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The stated target population was lung-transplant recipients with COPD, but the Methods describe identifying adult admissions solely through lung-transplant procedure codes and do not report a COPD-specific diagnostic inclusion criterion. “Chronic pulmonary disease” was instead retained as a predictor and was present in only a subset of the cohort, creating uncertainty about whether the analysed participants represent the stated COPD population.
	•	Predictors (Input Variable Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: Although seven overt post-discharge variables were removed, several retained predictors were contemporaneous or postoperative events, including acute kidney injury, sepsis, mechanical ventilation, ECMO, respiratory failure, and complications of the transplanted lung. The prediction time was not clearly defined, so some predictors may only become available after substantial hospital charges have already accumulated.
	•	Outcome (Definition Bias): Low Risk | Quote/Reasoning: Total hospital charges were obtained from the same administrative database for all admissions, and high cost was defined consistently as charges “greater than or equal to” the explicit 80th-percentile threshold of $600,223.40. The outcome was therefore objective and uniformly defined, although it represents charges rather than true economic costs.
	•	Analysis (Modeling Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The study used an internal test split and hyperparameter tuning, but there was no external validation and no AUC confidence interval. The paper does not state that MICE imputation, the 80th-percentile outcome threshold, and RFE feature selection were confined to the training data, creating potential leakage. The 20:80 outcome imbalance was not explicitly addressed, and calibration was presented graphically without calibration intercept or slope.
	•	Applicability to Review Question: Low Concern | Quote/Reasoning: The study directly evaluates machine-learning prediction in lung-transplant hospitalizations and addresses a prespecified transplantation-related healthcare-utilization outcome. Despite uncertainty regarding the COPD-specific cohort definition, it remains applicable to a review of AI models in thoracic organ transplantation.

